# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Business and industry sections."""

SECTIONS = [("BUSINESS", [
 ("h2", "Overview"),
 ("p", "ECNUchat, Inc. develops and operates an end-to-end encrypted messaging platform whose central design commitment is that <b>the operator of the service cannot read the messages it carries</b>. Our relay servers receive, store temporarily and forward sealed cryptographic envelopes. The keys that open those envelopes are generated on users’ devices, are never transmitted to us, and are mathematically unrecoverable from any data our servers hold. Confidentiality on our platform is therefore a property of its protocol design, verified by published cryptographic analysis and open-source code, rather than a consequence of our internal policies."),
 ("p", "The platform originated in 2026 as an academic research project in applied cryptography and has been developed into a complete messaging system: a protocol engine implementing post-quantum hybrid key establishment and continuously re-keyed message encryption; desktop and terminal clients sharing that engine; end-to-end encrypted group messaging, file transfer up to 8 GiB, voice messages and voice calls; and a relay server that stores only public keys, signed prekeys and sealed envelopes. Our codebase comprises approximately 20,000 lines of Python, and our protocol implementation is covered by an automated suite of 95 test functions, including adversarial tests against a simulated malicious server."),
 ("h2", "Industry Background"),
 ("p", "Instant messaging is the most-used communications medium in the world, and its security properties have become a matter of public policy. Three developments frame our market opportunity. First, the normalization of end-to-end encryption by large platforms has educated users to expect it, while repeated breaches, insider-access incidents and compelled-disclosure disputes have taught sophisticated users that operator-held keys and operator-readable content are liabilities. Second, the standardization of post-quantum cryptography — in particular NIST’s publication of FIPS 203 (ML-KEM) in August 2024 — started a multi-year migration clock: any communication that must remain secret for a decade or more must be protected today against adversaries recording ciphertext for future quantum decryption. Third, regulators and enterprise compliance regimes increasingly require demonstrable, auditable security architectures rather than policy assurances. We believe these forces create an opening for a platform whose security is structural, post-quantum from the first handshake and continuously thereafter, and independently verifiable."),
 ("h2", "Our Platform"),
 ("p", "The ECNUchat platform is organized in four layers, each with a strictly bounded role. The <b>cryptographic layer</b> (key agreement, ratcheting and prekey management) performs no network or database operations and consumes and produces only byte strings, which makes it independently testable. The <b>session engine</b> decides when to register keys, fetch prekey bundles, establish ratchets, fan out group messages and manage encrypted attachments. The <b>presentation layer</b> comprises a desktop application (built on PySide6, with three interface themes) and a terminal application, both driven by the same session engine, so security behavior cannot diverge between clients. The <b>relay layer</b> is a WebSocket server and store that holds public keys, signed prekeys, one-time prekeys, signed group states and offline envelopes, and that validates signatures before accepting key material — it never parses plaintext, because none ever reaches it."),
 ("h2", "Our Technology in Detail"),
 ("p", "<b>Identity and key system.</b> At registration, each client generates, on-device, separate long-term keys for separate purposes: an Ed25519 signing identity key (IK_sign), used only to authenticate; an X25519 agreement identity key (IK_dh); a signed X25519 prekey (SPK), whose signature the relay verifies before accepting it; a pool of one-time prekeys (OPKs), each deleted after a single use; and a post-quantum ML-KEM-768 key pair (PQK) together with one-time post-quantum prekeys (PQOPKs), signed by IK_sign. Private keys never leave the device and are stored in a local vault encrypted under a key derived from the user’s password using PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 with 600,000 iterations and AES-256-GCM. Usernames are unique; there is no email or telephone number in the system."),
 ("p", "<b>Authentication.</b> Login is a challenge–response protocol: the relay issues a 32-byte random challenge and the client signs it with IK_sign, proving possession of the registered identity key without transmitting any password. The relay holds no passwords at all."),
 ("p", "<b>Session establishment — X3DH extended to PQXDH.</b> To open a conversation, a client fetches the peer’s signed prekey bundle and performs the X3DH key agreement: four X25519 Diffie-Hellman operations (identity-to-signed-prekey, ephemeral-to-identity, ephemeral-to-signed-prekey and, when available, ephemeral-to-one-time-prekey), whose outputs are combined with HKDF-SHA256 under the domain separator “x3dh-v1” to yield the session’s initial secret. Following the PQXDH design of Fiedler and Günther (PKC 2025), we extend this handshake with an ML-KEM-768 encapsulation against the peer’s signed one-time or long-term post-quantum prekey; the KEM shared secret and a hash of the encapsulation key are bound into the HKDF input under the separate domain separator “pqxdh-v1,” and the initial message is signed over all of its components. Where both endpoints support the hybrid protocol, a classical-only handshake is refused, preventing silent downgrade; a relay that withholds one-time post-quantum prekeys can force fallback only to the peer’s signed long-term post-quantum key, a degradation the client surfaces to the user."),
 ("p", "<b>Message encryption — the Double Ratchet, made hybrid.</b> The session secret seeds the Double Ratchet rather than encrypting any message directly. A symmetric-key chain advances for every message (HMAC-SHA256 chain steps producing a unique message key and the next chain key), and a Diffie-Hellman ratchet step on every reply re-roots the chains via HKDF under the separator “dr-v1.” Every message is encrypted with AES-256-GCM under its own message key, with associated data binding the sender, recipient, message counters and the current ratchet public key, so ciphertext cannot be transplanted between conversations or positions. Message keys are deleted after use, providing forward secrecy; each completed reply restores security after a compromise, providing post-compromise (self-healing) security. In our hybrid extension, every new sending chain additionally mixes in a fresh ML-KEM-768 shared secret encapsulated to the peer’s long-term post-quantum key (domain separator “pq-dr-v1”), and chains lacking the post-quantum component are rejected once both endpoints are known to support it — so post-quantum protection is not a one-time property of session setup but is renewed with every ratchet step."),
 ("p", "<b>Out-of-order delivery, replay and tamper resistance.</b> Because networks reorder messages, the ratchet derives and caches message keys for skipped positions, capped at 40 keys per chain to prevent resource exhaustion; a cached key is consumed exactly once, making replays detectable and rejected. Decryption is performed against a copy of the ratchet state and committed only if AES-256-GCM authentication succeeds, so forged or tampered envelopes cannot burn future keys or corrupt the session."),
 ("p", "<b>Fingerprints and the malicious-server boundary.</b> A user’s identity fingerprint is the SHA-256 hash of the IK_sign public key, displayed as 16 groups of four hexadecimal digits for out-of-band comparison. Clients pin the first identity keys they see for each contact and refuse silent changes. Our shipped attack demonstrations show precisely what a malicious relay can and cannot do: without fingerprint verification it can substitute keys and interpose itself; with verification, substitution is detected and the relay is reduced to forwarding envelopes it cannot read, modify undetectably, or replay."),
 ("p", "<b>Group messaging.</b> Groups support up to eight members. Rather than distributing a shared sender key — the design whose future-secrecy weaknesses were documented by Rösler, Mainka and Schwenk (IEEE EuroS and P 2018) — our session engine encrypts each group message separately under the pairwise ratchet with each recipient, so every copy carries the full forward-secrecy and self-healing properties of a one-to-one conversation. Membership is governed by a signed, hash-chained group state: the genesis state is signed by the creator, each subsequent state adds exactly one member, links to its predecessor by hash and must be signed by an existing member, and every group message carries the hash of the state under which it was sent. A relay therefore cannot insert ghost members, present divergent membership lists to different members, or roll membership back without detection. Membership consent is recorded by the relay’s invitation mechanism; removal of members is a known limitation of the current monotonic chain design and is on our roadmap."),
 ("p", "<b>Attachments, voice messages and calls.</b> Files up to 8 GiB are encrypted under a per-file key in 8 MiB AES-256-GCM chunks, with chunk indices and file identifiers bound as associated data; the file key, digest and metadata travel inside the ratchet-encrypted message layer, and recipients verify every chunk and the whole-file SHA-256 digest before accepting content. Voice messages use the same chunked encryption. Voice calls are established by delivering a call key inside a ratchet envelope; media then flows as AES-256-GCM frames (40-millisecond cadence) relayed only while both parties are online, and the relay learns only that a call occurred and its volume."),
 ("p", "<b>Reliability engineering.</b> Sending is transactional: the advanced ratchet state and the complete envelope are committed to the local database in a single transaction before transmission, so a crash cannot desynchronize a session; failed sends are retried with the original envelope rather than by re-ratcheting. Offline envelopes are queued by the relay in order and deleted only upon confirmed delivery. Clients reconnect automatically and resume without re-establishment."),
 ("h2", "Security Model, Assumptions and Known Limitations"),
 ("p", "We state our security model as plainly in this prospectus as in our technical documentation, because our credibility with expert users depends on it. Our confidentiality and integrity guarantees rest on the published analyses of X3DH/PQXDH and the Double Ratchet, including their modeling assumptions; we do not claim new proofs for the composed system. Our implementation inherits the residual window identified in the CRYPTO 2022 analysis of the Double Ratchet, in which a sending ratchet private key must be retained until the peer’s next ratchet key arrives; the Triple Ratchet construction that closes this window is not implemented. Our relay learns communication metadata (participants, timing, envelope sizes); sealed-sender delivery and header encryption are not implemented. The platform is single-device per account at present. Group membership is append-only in the current signed-chain design. We disclose these limitations in our documentation and regard the roadmap items that address them — multi-device support, metadata protection, header encryption and member removal — as core development priorities rather than marketing claims."),
 ("h2", "Products, Pricing and Monetization Plan"),
 ("p", "The consumer platform is and will remain free of charge; charging individuals for basic private communication would contradict our mission and our growth strategy. Our planned revenue comes from organizations that need confidential communication as infrastructure: (i) <b>managed relay hosting</b>, in which an enterprise or institution operates a dedicated, audited relay for its members while keys remain on member devices; (ii) <b>compliance archiving under customer-held keys</b>, in which regulated organizations retain decryptable archives under keys they alone hold — an architecture only possible because archiving is an endpoint function in our design; and (iii) <b>premium infrastructure</b>, including high-availability relay capacity, priority delivery and administrative tooling. We will not sell advertising or user data; our architecture gives us no message content to sell, and we regard that constraint as a feature of the business model rather than a limitation of it."),
 ("h2", "Growth Strategy and Roadmap"),
 ("bullet", "<b>Complete the second-generation platform.</b> Our v2 architecture pairs the protocol engine with a web (PWA) client and a multi-relay design, reducing installation friction while preserving the same security core; real-world interoperability between our first-generation post-quantum implementation and v2 has been validated in testing."),
 ("bullet", "<b>Independent security evaluation.</b> We intend to commission third-party audits of the protocol implementation and to maintain a public vulnerability-disclosure and bug-bounty program, and to continue publishing attack demonstrations and test evidence with each release."),
 ("bullet", "<b>Close the documented gaps.</b> Multi-device support, sealed-sender metadata protection, header encryption and signed-chain member removal are specified roadmap items, in that order of security impact."),
 ("bullet", "<b>Grow through trust communities.</b> Journalists, legal professionals, healthcare providers, researchers and civil-society organizations have acute confidentiality needs and strong peer networks; we intend to seed adoption in these communities before broad consumer marketing."),
 ("bullet", "<b>Standards engagement.</b> We intend to participate in the post-quantum migration dialogue in standards and open-source communities, including alignment with IETF work on messaging layer security where our designs converge, so that verifiable post-quantum messaging becomes an industry expectation rather than a differentiator."),
 ("h2", "Competition"),
 ("p", "We compete with (i) global consumer platforms that bundle end-to-end encryption at no charge, including Signal, WhatsApp, Telegram (in its secret-chat mode), iMessage and WeChat; (ii) enterprise communications suites in which encryption keys are held or recoverable by the operator or the customer organization; and (iii) open-source and federated systems. We believe we compete on the verifiability and post-quantum completeness of our security architecture, on the structural inability of our operator to read content, and on organizational services built around customer-held keys. Many competitors have greater brand recognition, larger user bases, and substantially greater financial and technical resources than we do."),
 ("h2", "Intellectual Property"),
 ("p", "Our principal intellectual property assets are our codebase, our trademarks (including “ECNUchat” and our logo) and our accumulated protocol-engineering know-how. Our software is distributed under an open-source license; we rely on trademark, on the operational advantages of running the reference implementation and relay network, and on confidentiality around unreleased work, rather than on patents, of which we currently hold none. We intend to pursue trademark registrations in our principal markets."),
 ("h2", "Regulation"),
 ("p", "Our business is affected by laws and regulations governing cryptography, data protection, telecommunications and export controls in the jurisdictions in which we operate and offer service, including China’s Cryptography Law, Cybersecurity Law, Data Security Law and Personal Information Protection Law, the EU General Data Protection Regulation, and U.S. export controls on encryption technology. See “Risk Factors” for a discussion of how these regimes may affect us, including the possibility of lawful-access mandates that are incompatible with end-to-end encryption."),
 ("h2", "Employees and Organization"),
 ("p", "Our founding team comprises four engineers with combined expertise across cryptographic protocol implementation, client application development and relay infrastructure. We operate from Shanghai, China, with a development model built around open-source contribution and public security review. As of the date of this prospectus, all platform development has been performed by the founding team; we intend to expand our engineering, security-review and infrastructure headcount with the proceeds of this offering."),
 ("h2", "Facilities and Infrastructure"),
 ("p", "Our relay infrastructure runs on commodity cloud hosting and is designed so that no facility we operate ever holds message plaintext or user private keys. Our principal offices are in Shanghai, China. We do not own real property."),
 ("h2", "Legal Proceedings"),
 ("p", "We are not currently subject to any material legal proceedings, and we are not aware of any proceedings contemplated by governmental authorities that would be material to our business."),
])]

SECTIONS += [("INDUSTRY OVERVIEW", [
 ("p", "This section summarizes publicly available information regarding our industry. We have not independently verified third-party data, and our characterizations of market direction reflect trends we believe are material to our business rather than precise measurements of market size."),
 ("h2", "Messaging Is the Default Layer of Human Communication"),
 ("p", "Instant messaging has displaced email and voice calls as the primary medium of personal and much professional communication worldwide. The confidentiality of that medium is consequently a first-order social and commercial concern: negotiations, medical consultations, legal advice, journalism and intimate personal life all traverse messaging systems. In the dominant architectures, however, confidentiality has historically depended on the operator — on its key management, its internal access controls, its resistance to intrusion and its posture toward legal process. Each widely reported breach, insider-abuse case or compelled disclosure reprices that dependence."),
 ("h2", "End-to-End Encryption Became the Expectation"),
 ("p", "Over the past decade, end-to-end encryption moved from a specialist feature to a default expectation for consumer messaging, driven by the deployment of the Signal protocol family across major platforms. The research frontier correspondingly moved from whether to encrypt end to end, to the quality of the guarantees: forward secrecy, post-compromise security, group security, metadata exposure and — most recently — post-quantum resilience. Evaluated against the published literature, widely deployed systems still differ materially on these axes: group designs based on shared sender keys do not extend pairwise forward secrecy to group traffic; metadata protection is partial; and post-quantum protection, where deployed, has generally been applied at session establishment rather than continuously."),
 ("h2", "The Post-Quantum Migration Has a Deadline Set by Adversaries"),
 ("p", "NIST’s publication of FIPS 203 (ML-KEM) in August 2024, alongside FIPS 204 and FIPS 205, completed the first generation of post-quantum standards, and government migration guidance (including the U.S. Commercial National Security Algorithm Suite 2.0) sets transition timelines through the early 2030s. For messaging, however, the operative deadline is not the arrival of a quantum computer but the present day: an adversary who records ciphertext now can decrypt it whenever a cryptographically relevant quantum computer exists — the “harvest now, decrypt later” attack. Communications with long confidentiality horizons — legal, medical, journalistic, governmental and personal — are therefore already exposed unless their key establishment and ongoing key evolution are post-quantum hybrid. We believe this asymmetry between the visible state of deployment and the actual state of exposure is the central commercial fact of our market."),
 ("h2", "Regulation Is Moving Toward Demonstrable Architecture"),
 ("p", "Data protection regimes increasingly ask not merely what an operator promises but what its systems make possible: data minimization, purpose limitation and security-by-design are legal concepts in the EU and China alike, and procurement processes in regulated industries increasingly require auditable cryptographic architecture. At the same time, lawful-access proposals in several jurisdictions press in the opposite direction. We believe the tension between these forces raises the value of architectures in which the boundary of the technically possible is itself the compliance story."),
 ("h2", "Our Position"),
 ("p", "Within this landscape, we occupy a specific position: a messaging platform whose entire stack — establishment, ratcheting, groups, files and calls — is built from peer-reviewed constructions, is post-quantum hybrid at both establishment and every ratchet step, treats its own operator as a potential adversary in its published threat model, and is open source so that every one of those claims can be checked. Our commercial task is to convert that technical position into adoption and organizational revenue before incumbent platforms close the same gaps at their far greater scale."),
])]
